  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a diffraction or scattering experiment and the mathematical link between a sample’s internal structure and the measured pattern. A broad incoming wave, labeled with a plane-wave expression, strikes a small 3D sample. Inside the sample, the quantity shown as ρ(r) appears to represent the real-space density or structure of the object.\n\nFrom the sample, several scattered rays fan out toward a flat detector on the right. The detector is drawn with many blue spots of different sizes, suggesting a measured intensity pattern in reciprocal space. Above this, the equation for F(Δk) as an integral over ρ(r) indicates that the scattered wave amplitude is the Fourier transform of the sample density. At the bottom right, the intensity is written as I(Δk) = F(Δk) · F(Δk)*, showing that the detector records the squared magnitude of that complex amplitude rather than the amplitude itself.\n\nOverall, the image explains the core idea that illumination of a sample produces a diffraction pattern whose spot distribution encodes the sample structure through a Fourier transform, while the measured detector signal is intensity only.",
